        "summary": "4-Fluoroethylphenidate (commonly known as 4F-EPH) is a novel synthetic stimulant of the phenidate chemical class that produces long-lasting euphoric and stimulating effects associated with potent monoamine reuptake inhibitors when administered. It is a closely related structural analog of the commonly prescribed ADHD drug methylphenidate (known by the brand-names Ritalin and Concerta) as well as a designer drug analog ethylphenidate. Based on its similarities to other memebers of this class, it is speculated to exert its activity as some form of double or triple monoamine reuptake inhibitor.\n\n⚖️ Comparative profile: Fluorinated ethylphenidate analog providing stronger physical drive than methylphenidate with lower mood elevation than amphetamines.",
        "effectsSummary": "Wakefulness, increased conversational fluency, task motivation, moderate euphoria, and elevated heart rate.",
        "dosageRemark": "Standard doses: 10-25 mg orally or insufflated. Duration of active effects: 5-7 hours.",
        "generalRisks": "Tachycardia, vasoconstriction, nasal irritation from caustic salts, and post-peak anxiety.",
        "longtermRisks": "Psychological dependence, tolerance, and cardiovascular strain.",
        "saferUse": [
          "Prefer oral ingestion over nasal use to avoid mucosal damage.",
          "Do not consume after midday to protect sleep."
        ],
