        "summary": "Carbamazepine (Tégrétol) is an anticonvulsant, bipolar mood stabilizer, and first-line medication for trigeminal neuralgia. It is a powerful hepatic enzyme inducer that accelerates the clearance of dozens of other drugs.\n\n⚖️ Comparative profile: Sodium channel anticonvulsant and mood stabilizer effective for trigeminal neuralgia and bipolar disorder, requiring caution due to broad hepatic enzyme induction.",
        "effectsSummary": "Stabilizes neuronal sodium channels to prevent seizures, manic episodes, and paroxysmal trigeminal nerve pain; causes dizziness, ataxia, drowsiness, or double vision at peak levels.",
        "dosageRemark": "STRICT 8-12h SCHEDULE: Divided into 2 to 3 daily intakes with meals. The 'Heavy' threshold (400 mg) represents the maximum single immediate-release unit intake (daily totals up to 800-1200+ mg/24h are divided).",
        "generalRisks": "Dizziness, diplopia (double vision), ataxia, drowsiness, hyponatremia (low sodium), nausea, severe skin rash (SJS/TEN), hepatotoxicity, and leukopenia/agranulocytosis.",
        "longtermRisks": "Bone mineral density loss (vitamin D depletion), teratogenicity, hyponatremia, and persistent CYP450 enzyme induction.",
        "saferUse": [
          "NEVER combine with MAOIs (14-day washout) or Clozapine (fatal bone-marrow agranulocytosis!).",
          "NEVER consume Grapefruit juice (inhibits CYP3A4 and spikes Carbamazepine to toxic levels!).",
          "WARNING: Carbamazepine renders oral/implant Hormonal Birth Control ineffective and drastically lowers blood levels of Quetiapine, Aripiprazole, Risperidone, Alprazolam, Methadone, Buprenorphine, Oxycodone, Tramadol, and Lamotrigine!"
        ],
