        "summary": "Chlordiazepoxide (also known under the brand name Librium) is a classical long-acting benzodiazepine. Acting as a positive allosteric modulator of GABAA receptors, it produces anxiolytic, sedative, anticonvulsant, and muscle-relaxing effects, and is widely used for acute alcohol withdrawal.\n\n⚖️ Comparative profile: Prototypical first benzodiazepine, roughly half the potency of diazepam with very slow elimination, making it a reliable standard for alcohol detoxification tapers.",
        "effectsSummary": "Smooth anxiolysis, mild sedation, muscle relaxation, tremor reduction, and prevention of autonomic hyperexcitability and seizures.",
        "dosageRemark": "Standard anxiolytic dose: 10-25 mg 2-3 times daily. In alcohol detox: titrated up to 50-100 mg daily under medical supervision.",
        "generalRisks": "Daytime drowsiness, psychomotor impairment, ataxia, lightheadedness, and risk of accumulation in hepatic impairment or elderly patients.",
        "longtermRisks": "Physical and psychological dependence after sustained use, with severe withdrawal symptoms (rebound anxiety, delirium, seizures) upon abrupt discontinuation.",
        "saferUse": [
          "Strictly avoid alcohol and central depressants during therapy to prevent respiratory depression.",
          "Taper gradually under medical guidance when ceasing long-term regimens.",
          "Avoid driving or hazardous machinery during the initial titration phase."
        ],
