        "summary": "Creatine (and its derivatives hydrochloride, malate, nitrate, et al.) is an amino acid with nootropic effects that occurs naturally within the body of  vertebrates and in some foods such as meat, eggs, and fish. It was identified in 1832 when Michel Eugène Chevreul isolated it from the basified water-extract of skeletal muscle. He later named the crystallized precipitate after the Greek word for meat, κρέας (kreas). Early analysis showed that human blood is approximately 1% creatine.\n\n⚖️ Comparative profile: Well-researched cellular ATP buffer, enhancing muscular power output and mitigating cognitive fatigue during sleep deprivation.",
        "effectsSummary": "Increased muscular explosive power, enhanced resistance to mental and physical fatigue, faster cognitive processing under sleep deficit.",
        "dosageRemark": "Standard daily dose: 3-5 g taken consistently with water or carbohydrates. Loading phases (20 g/day for 5 days) are optional.",
        "generalRisks": "Benign intracellular water retention, mild stomach discomfort if taken without enough liquid.",
        "longtermRisks": "Extensive decades-long clinical safety record. Completely harmless to renal function in healthy individuals.",
        "saferUse": [
          "Maintain adequate daily hydration (2-2.5L water) to support cellular hydration.",
          "Take consistently every day; benefits stem from tissue saturation over 2-3 weeks."
        ],
