        "summary": "Desipramine (norimipramine, Pertofran / Norpramin) is the active N-demethylated metabolite of imipramine (Tofranil), formed via hepatic CYP2C19, CYP1A2, and CYP3A4, as well as a standalone tricyclic antidepressant. It is the most potent and selective norepinephrine transporter (NET) inhibitor among all tricyclics, cleared at ~85% via CYP2D6 2-hydroxylation (half-life ~22h, doubled in CYP2D6 poor metabolizers).\n\n⚖️ Comparative profile: More activating and less sedating/anticholinergic than imipramine or amitriptyline, but carries a narrow cardiac therapeutic index.",
        "effectsSummary": "Potent norepinephrine reuptake inhibition (NET), activating antidepressant effect, neuropathic analgesia, and moderate anticholinergic effects.",
        "dosageRemark": "Clinical dosage: 75 to 150 mg/day in divided or single daily doses (max 200 mg/day).",
        "generalRisks": "Dry mouth, sinus tachycardia, orthostatic hypotension, constipation, urinary retention, and cardiac arrhythmia risk in overdose or with CYP2D6 inhibitors.",
        "longtermRisks": "Moderate weight gain, cardiac conduction delays at high doses, and cholinergic rebound (sweating, nausea, insomnia) upon abrupt discontinuation.",
        "saferUse": [
          "Strictly contraindicated with MAOIs, stimulants (amphetamines, cocaine, MDMA), and tramadol.",
          "Reduce dose by 50% in CYP2D6 poor metabolizers or when co-administered with CYP2D6 inhibitors (fluoxetine, paroxetine, bupropion).",
          "Strictly adhere to prescribed doses: tricyclic overdose is a life-threatening cardiac emergency."
        ],
        "tolerance": {
          "full": "develops with prolonged and repeated use",
          "half": "3 - 7 days",
          "zero": "1 - 2 weeks"
        },
