        "summary": "Levetiracetam (Keppra) is a widely prescribed SV2A-binding anticonvulsant requiring strict 12-hour twice-daily dosing. Minimal hepatic drug interactions, but well-known for potential psychiatric side effects (irritability, anxiety, mood lability).\n\n⚖️ Comparative profile: Broad-spectrum anticonvulsant targeting vesicle protein SV2A, causing minimal sedation but potentially triggering mood irritability.",
        "effectsSummary": "Prevents focal and generalized seizures; may cause somnolence, fatigue, dizziness, or marked irritability/emotional lability.",
        "dosageRemark": "STRICT 12-HOUR INTERVAL: Must be taken twice daily every 12 hours (typically 500 mg to 1500 mg twice daily). The 'Heavy' threshold (1500 mg) is the maximum single unit intake (3000 mg/24h maximum).",
