        "summary": "🧬 ENDOGENOUS METABOLITE: Morphine-6-glucuronide (M6G) is the major active conjugated metabolite of morphine (and of heroin, codeine, and opium), formed at ~12% in the liver by UGT2B7 (while ~60% forms inactive M3G). Crucially, M6G is 1.5 to 2 times more potent than morphine itself at central µ-opioid receptors and relies almost exclusively on renal glomerular filtration (GFR) for clearance (t½ ~3.2h in normal renal function, rising to 15-50h in renal failure).\n\n⚖️ Comparative profile: Crosses the blood-brain barrier slowly but remains trapped in the CNS longer than morphine, accounting for up to 50% of analgesia and respiratory depression during repeated dosing.",
        "effectsSummary": "Potent, prolonged central µ-opioid analgesia, deep sedation, miosis, and delayed respiratory depression.",
        "dosageRemark": "🧬 Active endogenous metabolite of Morphine, Codeine, Heroin, and Opium (not marketed; internal reference tiers).",
        "generalRisks": "Delayed respiratory depression (especially with reduced renal function, dehydration, or NSAID co-use) and prolonged narcosis.",
        "longtermRisks": "Renal accumulation during chronic morphine dosing.",
        "saferUse": [
          "In renal impairment (eGFR < 50 mL/min) or elderly patients, M6G dangerously accumulates after repeated Morphine or Codeine doses.",
          "Avoid combining Morphine with NSAIDs in vulnerable individuals (NSAID-induced GFR reduction slows M6G clearance)."
        ],
