        "summary": "Nordiazepam (desmethyldiazepam, marketed as Nordaz, Calmday, or Madar) is both a standalone long-acting anxiolytic medication and the primary active metabolite of diazepam (Valium), clorazepate (Tranxene), prazepam (Lysanxia), and chlordiazepoxide (Librium). Acting as a partial positive allosteric modulator at GABA-A receptors, it exhibits an exceptionally long elimination half-life (40 to 100 hours, ~65h average) before 3-hydroxylation via CYP3A4 into oxazepam.\n\n⚖️ Comparative profile: Roughly 1.5x less potent by weight than diazepam (15 mg nordiazepam ≈ 10 mg diazepam on the Ashton scale), less sedating at peak, but drives multi-day plasma accumulation.",
