        "summary": "🧬 ENDOGENOUS METABOLITE: Norpethidine (normeperidine) is the N-demethylated metabolite of pethidine (meperidine / Demerol), formed at ~45% via CYP2B6 and CYP3A4 and cleared ~85% via renal filtration (GFR). While parent pethidine has a short ~3.5h half-life, norpethidine has a 5x longer half-life (~18h, and >35h in renal impairment), retains 50% analgesic potency, and exerts twice the CNS excitatory pro-convulsant and serotonergic neurotoxicity!\n\n⚖️ Comparative profile: Norpethidine accumulation is the exact clinical reason pethidine is contraindicated for repeated dosing (>24-48h) and in renal insufficiency.",
        "effectsSummary": "Weak residual analgesia accompanied by neurotoxic CNS excitation (jitters, hyperreflexia, myoclonus), serotonergic activity, and seizure threshold reduction.",
        "dosageRemark": "🧬 Endogenous neurotoxic metabolite of Pethidine (not marketed; internal reference tiers).",
        "generalRisks": "Myoclonus, agitation, generalized seizures (which naloxone does not reverse and may worsen), and fatal serotonin syndrome with MAOIs/SSRIs.",
        "longtermRisks": "Rapid cumulative neurotoxicity within 24-48h of repeated pethidine dosing.",
        "saferUse": [
          "ABSOLUTE HAZARD: Never use Pethidine repeatedly or in renal impairment (convulsant Norpethidine accumulation).",
          "Strictly contraindicated with MAOIs, SSRIs, SNRIs, and Tramadol."
        ],
