        "summary": "Prazepam is a prescription benzodiazepine acting as a positive allosteric modulator of GABA-A receptors. Slow-onset, very long-half-life benzodiazepine prodrug of desmethyldiazepam (nordazepam, half-life 36-200 hours!), widely prescribed in France/Europe as Lysanxia (10 mg, 40 mg, and oral drops) for anxiety and benzodiazepine tapering. Approximate potency equivalence: ~15-20 mg Prazepam ≈ 10 mg Diazepam. Do not combine with other CNS depressants.\n\n⚖️ Comparative profile: Benzodiazepine prodrug gradually converted to nordiazepam, delivering smooth all-day anxiety dampening with low euphoric abuse liability.",
        "effectsSummary": "Anxiolysis (anxiety relief), sedation, hypnosis (sleep induction), muscle relaxation, anticonvulsant action; at higher/diverted doses causes disinhibition, motor ataxia, slurred speech, and anterograde amnesia ('blackouts').",
        "dosageRemark": "DIVERSION-ADAPTED DOSAGES: Dosages reflect both therapeutic and diverted/recreational usage (~15-20 mg ≈ 10 mg Diazepam). Doses in the 'Strong'/'Heavy' range (>=30-60 mg) frequently cause amnesia, compulsive redosing, delusion of sobriety, and behavioral disinhibition.",
        "generalRisks": "Drowsiness, impaired driving/coordination, memory blackouts, paradoxical aggression or disinhibition, falls, and fatal respiratory arrest if combined with opioids, alcohol, GHB, or other sedatives.",
