        "summary": "S-Adenosyl-L-methionine (also called S-Adenosyl methionine, Ademethionine and commonly as SAMe and SAM-e) is a common cosubstrate involved in methyl group transfers, transsulfuration, and aminopropylation in biological organisms. SAMe is an amino acid methionine bound to an ATP molecule that circulates in the blood naturally and acts as a 'methyl donor'. A methyl group in chemistry is simply a carbon molecule (bound to some hydrogens), and donating a methyl group to other molecules can accelerate or preserve reactions in the body as a form of metabolic 'maintenance'.\n\n⚖️ Comparative profile: Endogenous methyl donor aiding monoamine neurotransmitter synthesis, supporting positive mood and joint comfort without sedation.",
        "effectsSummary": "Mood brightening, alleviation of emotional apathy, joint pain reduction, and hepatic antioxidant support.",
