        "summary": "Tilidine (also known as Tilidin and Valoron) is a synthetic opioid analgesic commonly used in Germany and other European countries for moderate to severe pain. It is an inactive prodrug that is metabolized in the liver to its active metabolite nortilidine, a potent mu-opioid receptor agonist. Preparations are frequently formulated in fixed combination with naloxone to deter intravenous and non-medical parenteral abuse.\n\n⚖️ Comparative profile: Step-2 synthetic opioid roughly 5 times less potent than oral morphine, co-formulated with naloxone to deter intravenous abuse.",
        "effectsSummary": "Effective analgesia, bodily warmth, physical and psychological relaxation, mild sedation, mood elevation, and anxiolysis.",
        "dosageRemark": "Standard medical dose: 50-100 mg every 6-8 hours (extended-release). Daily ceiling typically 400-600 mg tilidine.",
        "generalRisks": "Nausea, dizziness, drowsiness, constipation, diaphoresis, and life-threatening respiratory depression in overdose or polysubstance misuse.",
        "longtermRisks": "Substantial physical and psychological dependence, rapid tolerance, and classic opioid withdrawal upon cessation.",
        "saferUse": [
          "Do not crush or snort extended-release formulations containing naloxone, which precipitates immediate withdrawal.",
          "Never combine with alcohol, benzodiazepines, or other CNS depressants.",
          "Space therapeutic doses by at least 6 to 8 hours."
        ],
        "crossTolerances": [],
        "toxicities": [],
        "isApproved": false,
